T1  - Population-Based Geospatial and Molecular Epidemiologic Study of Tuberculosis Transmission Dynamics, Botswana, 2012-2016.
DO  - https://dx.doi.org/10.3201/eid2703.203840
A1  - Zetola, Nicola M
A1  - Moonan, Patrick K
A1  - Click, Eleanor
A1  - Oeltmann, John E
A1  - Basotli, Joyce
A1  - Wen, Xiao-Jun
A1  - Boyd, Rosanna
A1  - Tobias, James L
A1  - Finlay, Alyssa
A1  - Modongo, Chawangwa
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - Tuberculosis (TB) elimination requires interrupting transmission of Mycobacterium tuberculosis. We used a multidisciplinary approach to describe TB transmission in 2 sociodemographically distinct districts in Botswana (Kopanyo Study). During August 2012-March 2016, all patients who had TB were enrolled, their sputum samples were cultured, and M. tuberculosis isolates were genotyped by using 24-locus mycobacterial interspersed repetitive units-variable number of tandem repeats. Of 5,515 TB patients, 4,331 (79%) were enrolled. Annualized TB incidence varied by geography (range 66-1,140 TB patients/100,000 persons). A total of 1,796 patient isolates had valid genotyping results and residential geocoordinates; 780 (41%) patients were involved in a localized TB transmission event. Residence in areas with a high burden of TB, age <24 years, being a current smoker, and unemployment were factors associated with localized transmission events. Patients with known HIV-positive status had lower odds of being involved in localized transmission.
